# -*- mode:org; coding:utf-8 -*-
#+TITLE: windows-runner-quicklisp-deps — the Windows CI runner cannot quickload FiveAM (network) so test:*:windows fail
#+STATUS: TODO
#+PRIORITY: P2
#+TYPE: infra
#+DEPENDS: windows-runner-provisioning
#+BRANCH: infra-windows-runner-quicklisp-deps

* Context

On 2026-08-07, with the native Windows runner back online, several
Windows CI jobs failed NOT on clautolisp code but because Quicklisp on the
runner cannot fetch dependencies over the network:

    ; file: C:/msys64/home/PPBN02261/quicklisp/quicklisp/http.lisp
    ;     (ERROR "Unable to process HTTP header")

This aborts (ql:quickload "fiveam" ...) at the top of `make test-sbcl`, so
the FiveAM test systems never load → "compilation unit aborted; caught 1
fatal ERROR". Observed on test:clautolisp:windows (job 15764322028) and
earlier test:alfe:windows — both do a runtime ql:quickload of fiveam (+
bordeaux-threads / babel / trivial-gray-streams for alfe).

The same commit is GREEN on the macOS runner (fiveam already present in
its ~/quicklisp) and on Linux docker (deps baked into the image — see the
clautolisp-quicklisp-ci-deps rule). The gap is specific to the Windows
runner's Quicklisp/network.

* Problem

test:clautolisp:windows / test:alfe:windows can never be trusted while the
Windows runner must reach the Quicklisp CDN at job time: the fetch is
flaky (TLS "unexpected eof", "Unable to process HTTP header"). They are
allow_failure, so they do not block, but they never give a real signal
either.

Jobs that do NOT ql-fetch pass fine on the same runner, proving the code
and toolchain are healthy: conformance:pathname-mapping:windows and
test:autolisp-test:windows were GREEN on the very same pipeline.

* Proposal

Provision the Windows runner's Quicklisp the way the Dockerfile / macOS
runner already are — bake the fixed dep set into ~/quicklisp so no
job-time download is needed:
  - fiveam, bordeaux-threads, babel, trivial-gray-streams (the set the
    Makefiles quickload), plus whatever alfe's spec-coverage adds.
  - i.e. run one `(ql:quickload ...)` of that set at provisioning time (or
    ship a local-projects / bundle), then jobs run fully offline.
Alternatively, point the runner at a local Quicklisp mirror.

* Acceptance criteria

- test:clautolisp:windows and test:alfe:windows run to a real
  pass/fail on the Windows runner with NO network fetch at job time.
- Re-run the current master pipeline: those two go green (the code is
  already green everywhere the fetch is not needed).

* Notes

Runner-provisioning task (owner: pjb / the Windows box), not a clautolisp
source change. Filed per the "write tickets for bugs found in passing"
rule while confirming the 1.8.7 pathname fix.
